r-issue severity, area-of-law, source citation, and recommendation. The proven shape mirrors risk-analysis but with citation as a first-class output: per-section :map-each with :output-schemas requiring :citation field, then :code (NOT :llm) for the severity-summary rollup. Always require citation in the schema so users can verify the model's flags against the source.\n\nCapabilities:\n  - flags legal concerns in a document — per-issue severity + area-of-law + source citation + recommendation\n  - produces :issues (vector with per-item structure) + :severity-summary (rolled-up counts by severity)\n  - FLAG-LOUDER variant of risk-analysis: citations back to source AND recommendations are first-class outputs\n  - natural decomposition: per-section issue identification, then aggregation, then severity rollup\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** per-section :map-each with structured :output-schemas reliably surfaces issues with consistent severity + citation shape (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: document has natural sections (paragraphs, clauses) AND each section can be independently scanned for issues\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:chunk-document {:from :document :delimiter \"\\n\\n\" :into :sections}] [:map-each {:from :sections :as :section :into :results :max-concurrency 3} [:llm {:reads [:section] :writes [:section-issues] :output-schemas {:section-issues [:vector [:map [:concern :string] [:severity [:enum :high :medium :low]] [:area-of-law :string] [:citation :string] [:recommendation :string]]]}}]] [:aggregate {:from :results :writes [:issues]}] [:code {:reads [:issues] :writes [:severity-summary] :fn (fn [{:keys [issues]}] {:severity-summary (frequencies (map :severity issues))})}] [:final {:keys [:issues :severity-summary]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** issues without citation fields become 'recommendations the user can't verify' — model needs to be explicitly required to cite (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the per-section :llm's :output-schemas does NOT include a :citation field\n    - Recommended fix: always include :citation in the :output-schemas + word the instruction to require quoting the source phrase that triggered the issue\n\n  - **Failure mode:** severity-summary computed via :llm wastes tokens — it's pure :code work (frequencies over a vector) (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the severity-summary step is a separate :llm call instead of a :code transform\n    - Recommended fix: use a :code node with (fn [{:keys [issues]}] {:severity-summary (frequencies (map :severity issues))}) — deterministic, fast, zero tokens\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - compliance review of a draft regulation or policy\n  - legal due diligence of a contract or agreement\n  - audit of a corporate document for liability exposure\n\n#### Alternative #1 — Legal-issue-detection (confidence: 1.00)\nWhy this fits: Matches the structural requirements and domain perfectly, providing a schema-driven approach for identifying legal concerns with citations.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nLegal-issue-detection trees flag legal concerns in a document with per-issue severity, area-of-law, source citation, and recommendation. The proven shape mirrors risk-analysis but with citation as a first-class output: per-section :map-each with :output-schemas requiring :citation field, then :code (NOT :llm) for the severity-summary rollup. Always require citation in the schema so users can verify the model's flags against the source.\n\nCapabilities:\n  - flags legal concerns in a document — per-issue severity + area-of-law + source citation + recommendation\n  - produces 